WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
Q-learning.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.643
A table, one update rule, and enough practice.

3
00:00:04.643 --> 00:00:07.267
That is all it takes to learn good behavior.

4
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.667
After each step, we nudge the estimate.

5
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:16.767
The target is the reward we actually got, plus the best value we believe is available next.

6
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:22.667
The gap between that target and our old estimate is the TD error: promise versus evidence.

7
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.133
Alpha, the learning rate, controls how big the nudge is.

8
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:28.333
Here is the loop.

9
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.551
With probability epsilon, explore randomly.

10
00:00:31.551 --> 00:00:34.333
Otherwise, exploit the best known action.

11
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.533
Take the step, and observe the reward and next state.

12
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:39.587
Apply the nudge, and repeat.

13
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:44.533
It is off-policy: it bootstraps from the best next action even while exploring.

14
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.567
After training, the Q-table looks like a heat grid.

15
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:53.033
Rows are states, columns are actions, and brightness is learned value.

16
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.500
Read a row's brightest cell, and you are reading the policy.

17
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.900
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.400
We choose an action epsilon greedily.

19
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.867
Then we compute the best next value, and nudge the table toward the target.

20
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.667
Run this on the Mars grid.

21
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:12.031
Two thousand episodes finish in under a second.

22
00:01:12.031 --> 00:01:19.267
The reward climbs from minus one, falling into the crater, to about plus zero point nine four: a clean beeline.

23
00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:21.633
Now make it visible.

24
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:23.300
Log the reward curve.

25
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.700
Plot the Q-table as a heat grid at episodes zero, one hundred, and five hundred.

26
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.565
You literally watch value flow backward from the lander.

27
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:35.367
That is the best RL intuition you can build.

28
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:37.360
Let's recap.

29
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.733
Nudge Q toward the reward plus the best next value.

30
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.567
The gap is the TD error.

31
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:45.867
It is off-policy, behaving epsilon greedily.

32
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:49.367
Swap the table for a network and you get DQN.

33
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:50.433
Next up: SARSA.
